---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const templates = [
  '01_simple_chat.py',
  '02_document_qa.py',
  '03_simple_agent.py',
  'bedrock_chat_preview.py',
];
const code: Record<string, string> = {};
for (const t of templates) {
  const fp = path.join(process.cwd(), 'sandbox', 'templates', t);
  code[t] = fs.readFileSync(fp, 'utf-8');
}

const defaultDoc = `Artificial intelligence is the simulation of human intelligence by machines.
A large language model (LLM) predicts the next token in a sequence using patterns learned from large amounts of text during training.

Transformers are the architecture behind modern LLMs. They use an attention mechanism so every token can look at every other token in the input.

An agent is a system that uses a model inside a loop: set a goal, plan steps, call tools, read the results, and decide whether to continue or stop. The model is the reasoning engine; the tools are the hands.`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sandbox — vaathi.dev"
  description="Build and run an AI agent in the browser. Watch every step animate: brief, model, tool call, result, answer."
>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="sandbox-heading">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <p class="mono-label">Stage 03 · Sandbox</p>
      <h1 id="sandbox-heading" class="section__title">Build, run, watch every step</h1>
      <p class="section__lede">
        After <a href="/learn/build">Build (Stage 2)</a>, run the same flow here.
        Pick a scenario, change the inputs, watch the animated pipeline — then read the annotated Python for that option.
      </p>

      <aside class="sb-stage-note" aria-label="How runs are secured">
        <p>
          <strong>Runs on Amazon Bedrock</strong> (Nova Micro, EU) through an AWS Lambda in your account —
          not OpenAI, no API key to paste. The browser only talks to the sandbox API; the model stays on the AWS backbone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Soft site cap ~$1/day (DynamoDB ledger) plus the AWS Budget on Bedrock. Cognito sign-in can gate this further next.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <!-- Scenario tabs -->
      <nav class="sb-tabs" aria-label="Scenario picker">
        <button class="sb-tab sb-tab--active" data-scenario-tab="chat" type="button">
          <span class="sb-tab__icon" aria-hidden="true">💬</span> Simple Chat
        </button>
        <button class="sb-tab" data-scenario-tab="rag" type="button">
          <span class="sb-tab__icon" aria-hidden="true">📄</span> Document Q&amp;A
        </button>
        <button class="sb-tab" data-scenario-tab="agent" type="button">
          <span class="sb-tab__icon" aria-hidden="true">🤖</span> Agent + Tools
        </button>
      </nav>

      <!-- ── Chat ── -->
      <div data-scenario-panel="chat" class="sb-panel">
        <p class="sb-panel__desc">
          One model call. You send a system prompt and a question. The model returns a single reply — no tools, no loop.
        </p>

        <label class="sandbox-label" for="sandbox-prompt">System prompt</label>
        <textarea id="sandbox-prompt" class="sandbox-code" rows="3">You are a helpful assistant that explains AI concepts in one short paragraph.</textarea>
        <label class="sandbox-label" for="sandbox-question-chat">User question</label>
        <textarea id="sandbox-question-chat" class="sandbox-code" data-role="question" rows="2">What is a large language model?</textarea>

        <div class="sb-explain">
          <h2 class="sb-explain__title">What this option does</h2>
          <ol class="sb-explain__steps">
            <li><strong>Create a client</strong> — loads your API key from the environment.</li>
            <li><strong>Send two messages</strong> — a <em>system</em> role (how to behave) and a <em>user</em> role (the question).</li>
            <li><strong>One completion</strong> — the model returns text. No tool calls, no second round.</li>
            <li><strong>Print the answer</strong> — you see the model&rsquo;s content field.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="sb-explain__bridge">
            In the browser run above, the same shape is used: system + user → stream tokens into the pipeline.
            On Bedrock later, this becomes a single <code>Converse</code> call to Nova Micro.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="sb-codeblock">
          <h3 class="sb-codeblock__title">Python — <code>01_simple_chat.py</code></h3>
          <dl class="sb-annotate">
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>from openai import OpenAI</code></dt>
              <dd>Import the official SDK. One client talks to the Chat Completions API.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>client = OpenAI()</code></dt>
              <dd>Reads <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> from the environment. No key in the source file.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>chat.completions.create(...)</code></dt>
              <dd>
                The actual call. <code>model</code> picks the model; <code>messages</code> is the brief;
                <code>max_tokens</code> caps length.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>role: system</code> / <code>role: user</code></dt>
              <dd>
                System = instructions. User = the question you type above. Change either field and re-run to feel the difference.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>response.choices[0].message.content</code></dt>
              <dd>The model&rsquo;s text reply. One choice, one message — the simplest possible result.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <pre class="sb-codeblock__pre"><code>{code['01_simple_chat.py']}</code></pre>

          <h3 class="sb-codeblock__title sb-codeblock__title--follow">Next stage — same idea on Bedrock (no user key)</h3>
          <p class="sb-explain__bridge sb-explain__bridge--pad">
            Lambda holds the IAM role. The browser calls your Function URL after Cognito auth — never OpenAI, never a pasted key.
          </p>
          <pre class="sb-codeblock__pre"><code>{code['bedrock_chat_preview.py']}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── RAG ── -->
      <div data-scenario-panel="rag" class="sb-panel" hidden>
        <p class="sb-panel__desc">
          The document is given as context. The model should answer only from that text — the idea behind RAG (retrieval-augmented generation).
        </p>

        <label class="sandbox-label" for="sandbox-doc">Document</label>
        <textarea id="sandbox-doc" class="sandbox-code" rows="6">{defaultDoc}</textarea>
        <label class="sandbox-label" for="sandbox-question-rag">Question about the document</label>
        <textarea id="sandbox-question-rag" class="sandbox-code" data-role="question" rows="2">What is the role of the model inside an agent?</textarea>

        <div class="sb-explain">
          <h2 class="sb-explain__title">What this option does</h2>
          <ol class="sb-explain__steps">
            <li><strong>Paste a document</strong> — your source of truth (policy, notes, article).</li>
            <li><strong>Split into chunks</strong> — small overlapping pieces so retrieval can find the right bit.</li>
            <li><strong>Embed &amp; store</strong> — each chunk becomes a vector in a FAISS index (similar meaning ≈ nearby vectors).</li>
            <li><strong>Retrieve + ask</strong> — pull the top chunks, then ask the model with that context.</li>
            <li><strong>Answer from sources</strong> — the chain returns the answer and the chunks it used.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="sb-explain__bridge">
            The browser demo simplifies step 2–3: it sends the whole document in the system prompt (fine for short text).
            The Python below is the real RAG pattern you would ship. On Bedrock, embeddings and the chat model both move to AWS.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="sb-codeblock">
          <h3 class="sb-codeblock__title">Python — <code>02_document_qa.py</code></h3>
          <dl class="sb-annotate">
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code></dt>
              <dd>Cuts the document into ~200-character pieces with overlap so sentences are not lost at boundaries.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>OpenAIEmbeddings</code> + <code>FAISS</code></dt>
              <dd>Turns each chunk into a vector and stores them locally. Search finds the chunks closest to the question.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>RetrievalQA</code></dt>
              <dd>Glue: retrieve <code>k=2</code> chunks → feed them to the chat model → return answer + source docs.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>qa.invoke(&#123;"query": ...&#125;)</code></dt>
              <dd>One call runs retrieve + generate. Print <code>result</code> and the retrieved chunks to verify the answer.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <pre class="sb-codeblock__pre"><code>{code['02_document_qa.py']}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Agent ── -->
      <div data-scenario-panel="agent" class="sb-panel" hidden>
        <p class="sb-panel__desc">
          The model can call a <code>lookup_fact</code> tool, read the result, and loop until it answers.
          Topics: <em>LLM, transformer, agent, embedding, RAG, MCP, AI, prompt</em>.
        </p>

        <label class="sandbox-label" for="sandbox-question-agent">Your question</label>
        <textarea id="sandbox-question-agent" class="sandbox-code" data-role="question" rows="2">What is an agent and how does it relate to an LLM?</textarea>

        <div class="sb-explain">
          <h2 class="sb-explain__title">What this option does</h2>
          <ol class="sb-explain__steps">
            <li><strong>Define a tool</strong> — <code>lookup_fact(topic)</code> returns a short fact from a fixed dictionary.</li>
            <li><strong>Bind the tool to the model</strong> — the model may request that tool instead of answering immediately.</li>
            <li><strong>Loop</strong> — model → (optional) tool call → tool result → model again, up to 5 steps.</li>
            <li><strong>Stop</strong> — when there are no more tool calls, the model&rsquo;s text is the final answer.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="sb-explain__bridge">
            The animated cards above are this loop made visible. That loop <em>is</em> the agent.
            Bedrock&rsquo;s Converse API supports the same tool-use pattern with a site key behind Cognito.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="sb-codeblock">
          <h3 class="sb-codeblock__title">Python — <code>03_simple_agent.py</code></h3>
          <dl class="sb-annotate">
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>@tool</code> / <code>lookup_fact</code></dt>
              <dd>Registers a function the model can call. The docstring tells the model <em>when</em> to use it.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>llm.bind_tools([lookup_fact])</code></dt>
              <dd>Attaches the tool schema to the model so responses can include structured tool calls.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>for step in range(5):</code></dt>
              <dd>Hard step limit — agents must stop. Without a limit, a buggy loop can spend forever.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>response.tool_calls</code></dt>
              <dd>If empty → final answer. If present → run each tool, append <code>ToolMessage</code>, continue the loop.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-annotate__row">
              <dt><code>ToolMessage(...)</code></dt>
              <dd>Feeds the tool&rsquo;s return value back so the next model turn can use real data, not guesses.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <pre class="sb-codeblock__pre"><code>{code['03_simple_agent.py']}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Run + pipeline -->
      <div class="sandbox-bar">
        <button id="sandbox-run" class="site-cta" type="button">▶ Run</button>
        <span id="sandbox-status" class="sandbox-status"></span>
      </div>
      <div id="sandbox-pipeline" class="sb-pipeline" aria-live="polite"></div>

      <!-- Guide -->
      <div class="sandbox-guide">
        <h2>How it works</h2>

        <h3>The animated pipeline</h3>
        <p>Every run shows cards as steps happen:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Brief</strong> — prompt / question / document context.</li>
          <li><strong>Model</strong> — tokens stream in.</li>
          <li><strong>Tool call</strong> — (agent) the model asks for a tool.</li>
          <li><strong>Tool result</strong> — the tool returns data.</li>
          <li><strong>Answer</strong> — final reply.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>On Agent + Tools, steps 2–4 repeat. That loop is the agent.</p>

        <h3>Security model</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Amazon Bedrock</strong> (Nova Micro, EU inference profile) — invoked only from Lambda IAM role.</li>
          <li><strong>No BYOK</strong> — nothing leaves the browser except the scenario payload to your Function URL.</li>
          <li><strong>Origin allow-list</strong> — Lambda rejects requests that are not from vaathi.dev (or local dev).</li>
          <li><strong>$1/day soft ledger</strong> + AWS Budget on Bedrock as the hard backstop.</li>
          <li><strong>Next:</strong> Cognito + TOTP so only signed-in learners can run.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Lessons ↔ Sandbox</h3>
        <table class="sandbox-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Build (Stage 2)</th><th>Foundations</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Simple Chat</td>
              <td><a href="/learn/build/calling-a-model">Calling a model</a></td>
              <td><a href="/learn/foundations/prompting-clear-communication">Prompting</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Document Q&amp;A</td>
              <td><a href="/learn/build/vectors-in-code">Vectors in code</a></td>
              <td><a href="/learn/foundations/why-vectors-embeddings">Embeddings</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Agent + Tools</td>
              <td>
                <a href="/learn/build/assemble-a-python-agent">Assemble</a>,
                <a href="/learn/build/grow-chat-into-agent">Chat→agent</a>
              </td>
              <td><a href="/learn/foundations/agentic-workflows">Agentic</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><a href="/learn/build">Start Stage 2 — Build path →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline src="/sandbox-config.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="/sandbox.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
